# Every iteration of an unrolled loop trusts what the loop's entry checks have found. With testing=CheckBoundsInEmittersOnly the walk adds nothing,
# the loop is unrolled as in an executable, and each access checks the facts that it was given against memory.
# The entry checks test whether the bound exceeds the length of an array that is read or stored into at the counter. For an array of ten elements and sixteen iterations, the loop as written runs.
# Fault injection, testing=SkipUnrollEntryChecks: the eleventh iteration reads past the end of the array.
# RUN(COUNT): compile mechanisms=all UnrolledLoopLengthChecksHoisted
# Each unrolled loop checks two lengths. The loop has a specialized version for Int32 storage, which is unrolled once, and one for doubles, which is unrolled twice.
# COUNT: statistic UnrolledLoopLengthChecksHoisted 6
# RUN(RIGHT): run-in-shell mechanisms=all globalThis.fails = true;
# RIGHT: 16:undefined
# RIGHT-NEXT: exited with 0
# RUN(HELD): run-in-shell mechanisms=all testing=CheckBoundsInEmittersOnly globalThis.fails = true;
# HELD: 16:undefined
# HELD-NEXT: exited with 0
# The injected fault does not harm an input that would have passed the entry checks. So the crash in the last run comes from this check and from no other.
# RUN(PASSES): run-in-shell mechanisms=all testing=CheckBoundsInEmittersOnly,SkipUnrollEntryChecks
# PASSES: 16:15
# PASSES-NEXT: exited with 0
# RUN(LIE): run-in-shell mechanisms=all testing=CheckBoundsInEmittersOnly,SkipUnrollEntryChecks globalThis.fails = true;
# LIE: bun: internal AOT error: redundancy elimination check failed
# LIE-NEXT: a load or check that the compiler removed as redundant gives a different result here
